# Project Overview
- Godot 4.5 bullet-hell shmup (320x180) with CRT aesthetics and integer scaling.
- Event-driven architecture via autoload singletons; enemies are component-based.
- Data-driven content (templates/configs); performance relies on pooling and safe scene ops.

# High-Level Goals for the AI
- Prefer minimal, safe edits that fit the current architecture and patterns.
- Maintain retro visual style and bullet readability at all times.
- Preserve event-driven decoupling; avoid tight coupling or ad-hoc cross-calls.
- Default to safe operations and guard-clauses; never crash on nulls or bad data.

# Must-Follow Principles
- Composition over inheritance for enemies/behaviors.
- Use signals and `EventBus` for cross-system communication.
- Use `EntityFactory` for all spawns (player, enemies, bullets) with object pooling.
- Use `GameState` for centralized player/game state; do not duplicate state.
- Use Template Managers (`EnemyTemplateManager`, `BossTemplateManager`, `StageTemplateManager`).
- Use `@export` for inspector-tunable values; keep GDScript types explicit.
- For scene-tree modifications, prefer `call_deferred()` when needed.
- Always check `is_instance_valid()` before using nodes; prefer `get_node_or_null()`.
- Bounds-check arrays; return safe defaults for missing data.

# When Unsure
- Read the relevant manager/system code; follow its existing patterns.
- Ask clarifying questions only if blocked; otherwise choose conservative, reversible edits.

# Do / Don’t
- Do keep diffs small and targeted; update related docs/configs when behavior changes.
- Do keep performance in mind (pooling, rate limiters, low allocation per frame).
- Don’t introduce synchronous heavy loops per frame; cache references and reuse.
- Don’t bypass factories or managers to “just make it work.”